KZ3 patch 1131 (SPU pass 110; upstream candidate): fi, frest, frsqest and the per-lane-count word shifts shl, rot,
rotm and rotma in SSE4.1 (AVX2 per-lane shifts when built with -mavx2). The helpers were per-lane loops that GCC does
not vectorize (a table lookup, a branch, a variable shift count): each wrote its four result lanes to the stack and
the next instruction read the 16-byte vector back, a failed store-to-load forward on x86 (~12-15 cycles per helper
call, visible in the profile as the instruction after each loop). In K4 (frozen_shores, spu-r110-t1, KZ3_PROF) these
loops and their reload stalls were ~35 % of the samples of the 3-bone skinning job's hottest function (job_01242c80
func_5868, 6.2 % of all SPU samples); fi/frest are in the hot functions of jobs 241, 242 and others too.

The new versions run the same operations on four lanes (fi: the same double scale, one cvtpd2ps rounding, float
mul/sub without contraction; frest/frsqest: the exponent arithmetic in vector form, the four table entries gathered by
index straight into a vector; shifts: x86 shifts give 0 / the sign for counts >= 32, the SPU result for 32..63). The
loops stay as spu__*_ref (fi's out of line, for patch 1071's exponent-255 lanes and builds without SSE4.1).
Same bits: tools/spu/test_r110_vec.sh (frest/frsqest on all 2^32 inputs, fi and the shifts on random and edge words,
SSE4.1 and AVX2 builds, with a negative control).

--- a/runtime/spu/spu_helpers.h
